---
title: App Setup
sidebar:
  label: App Setup
  order: 2
---

Prepare and test NAHPU on each device before relying on it for collection records. NAHPU is designed to make setup easy and config sharing straightforward. **Setup is where error prevention is cheapest.** A constraint added in Settings before departure removes a class of errors that would otherwise be found, if at all, by a curator years later.

{% bpdiagram kind="setup" locale="en" /%}

## Agree on the configuration

**Settle the configuration with the team, not device by device.** Dedicate one person to run [`Setup NAHPU`](/en/usages/setup/) on a lead device — choosing the catalog format, Field ID and Tissue ID conventions, controlled vocabularies for sites, events, specimens, and symbionts, and bundled export presets. At the `Finish` step, select `Export user configs` and share the file with the team. Everyone else selects `Import user configs` on the `Welcome` step of their own `Setup NAHPU`, then skips the rest of the wizard.

**Review field visibility and custom fields in [Settings](/en/usages/settings/).** Hidden fields can still hold data and appear in exports. Define a custom field only when a built-in field does not express the observation, and agree on its placement, type, scope, applicable catalog, units, and allowed values. Enter a test value and check its export before leaving camp or the lab.

{% aside type="tip" title="Worked example: configuring a four-person mammal crew" %}
Before a three-week survey, the field lead runs `Setup NAHPU` on the lead tablet: selects `Mammalogy`, turns on `Personnel ID`, sets a `Tissue ID` prefix (`PT-T`), trims `Specimen Types` and `Treatments` to the museum's list, turns on `I will collect symbionts`, and adds the mammal export presets. They export `user_configs.json` and send it with the project QR code to three teammates. Each teammate imports the config file on `Welcome`, scans the project QR, and starts with identical vocabularies and the same project UUID.
{% /aside %}

## Prepare shared resources

- **Personnel and taxa.** Assign the required personnel and review the taxon list, including identification ranks, uncertain identifications, and expected symbiont taxa. Reuse existing database entries when they represent the same person or taxon.
- **One maintainer.** Decide which device maintains shared definitions and distributes updates.
- **Documents, fonts, and offline maps.** Prepare export presets, document templates, print layouts, custom map layers (`GeoJSON`, zipped WGS84 Shapefiles, or `PMTiles` under `Settings` > `Sites` > `Map layers`), and required fonts. User-config transfers do not include custom font files, template images, or map files; install those assets separately and test the resulting documents and maps.
- **Project identity.** Use [project identity](/en/how-to-recipes/prepare/share-project-identity/) (`Export info` or `Scan project QR`) to establish a common project UUID when several devices contribute to one project. Everyone else imports that identity before creating records. A project name alone does not establish a shared UUID.

## Rehearse the workflow

Use a clearly named practice project (for example `Practice_2026`). Create a site, coordinate, event, and specimen; change an identification; add a part, a symbiont examination, media, and an associated file where applicable. **Leave and reopen each record to confirm the values persisted.** Dialogs need an explicit confirmation button; many record fields save automatically.

Then run the exercise the way the field will run it:

- Turn off Wi-Fi and cellular data, and verify location, camera, and barcode-scanning permissions work offline.
- Check an actual exported table and print a sample label to verify clipping, font rendering, and QR scanning.
- Import a project export on a separate test installation and open its records and attachments.
- Test database replacement only on an installation whose contents you can discard.

## Verify practice records against a physical tag

**Reopen each practice record and compare it against a sample physical tag, field by field.** Scanning an exported table for empty cells or outliers is not enough: almost every real data-entry error is a plausible value in an allowed range, so it slips past visual skimming. For identifiers and measurements that cannot be recovered once a specimen is in a jar, double-checking against the physical tag is worth the extra minutes.

{% aside type="note" title="Evidence for checking against the source" %}
[Barchard & Pace (2011)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2011.04.004) had 195 participants enter 30 data sheets by three methods. Only 0.06% of the resulting errors were blank or outside the allowable range for their variable. Visual checking produced 29–58% more errors than double entry and was no better than single entry, while double entry cost 33% more time.
{% /aside %}

## Before departure

Confirm available storage, power banks, installed resources, backup destinations, and the team's working project. See [Devices](/en/usages/devices/), [Device Choice and Care](/en/best-practices/device-care/), and [Backups and Recovery](/en/best-practices/backups-and-recovery/).

## References

Barchard KA & Pace LA (2011). [Preventing human error: the impact of data entry methods on data accuracy and statistical results](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2011.04.004). *Computers in Human Behavior* 27:1834–1839.

Chapman AD (2005). [Principles of Data Quality](https://www.gbif.org/document/80509/principles-of-data-quality). GBIF Secretariat, Copenhagen.
